## Browsing the web

You have two browser tools. Pick the right one for the job:

| Tool | When to use |
|---|---|
| **dev-browser** (CLI) | Browsing as Nityesh — anything that needs his logged-in sessions (X/Twitter, Stripe, Render, admin panels, etc.) |
| **agent-browser** (skill) | General browser use — debugging UI issues, testing pages, scraping public sites, anything that doesn't need Nityesh's auth |

### dev-browser — Nityesh's logged-in Chrome

`dev-browser` connects to Chrome via CDP (Chrome DevTools Protocol) and runs sandboxed JS with full Playwright Page API access. It uses a copy of Nityesh's Chrome profile, so all his sessions/cookies are available.

**Setup (run once per session — Chrome must be restarted with CDP enabled):**

```bash
# 1. Kill any running Chrome
pkill -f "Google Chrome"; sleep 3

# 2. Copy Nityesh's Chrome profile to a separate directory
mkdir -p /tmp/chrome-debug-profile
cp -a "$HOME/Library/Application Support/Google/Chrome/Default" /tmp/chrome-debug-profile/
cp -a "$HOME/Library/Application Support/Google/Chrome/Local State" /tmp/chrome-debug-profile/

# 3. Launch Chrome with remote debugging + extensions disabled
#    (extensions disabled because a site-blocker extension blocks some sites)
/Applications/Google\ Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google\ Chrome \
  --remote-debugging-port=9222 \
  --user-data-dir="/tmp/chrome-debug-profile" \
  --disable-extensions \
  &>/dev/null &

# 4. Verify CDP is listening
sleep 5 && curl -s http://localhost:9222/json/version
```

**Why the profile copy?** Chrome refuses `--remote-debugging-port` on the default data directory. Copying the profile to `/tmp/chrome-debug-profile` preserves sessions while satisfying Chrome's requirement.

**Usage:**

```bash
# Navigate and screenshot
echo '
const page = await browser.getPage("main");
await page.goto("https://x.com/home");
await page.waitForTimeout(3000);
await page.screenshot({ path: "screenshot.png" });
' | dev-browser --connect

# Screenshots are saved in ~/.dev-browser/tmp/
cp ~/.dev-browser/tmp/screenshot.png /tmp/screenshot.png
```

**Key constraints:**
- Screenshots must use **relative paths** (sandbox restriction) — files end up in `~/.dev-browser/tmp/`
- Always use `--connect` flag to attach to the running Chrome instance
- The profile copy is a snapshot — if Nityesh logs into something new in his main Chrome, re-copy the profile

### agent-browser — general browser use

Use the `agent-browser` skill for tasks that don't need Nityesh's login: debugging CC's UI, testing public pages, general web scraping. It launches its own browser instance via Playwright.

## Google Workspace (`gws`)

You have `gws` installed — a CLI for all Google Workspace APIs. You manage four Gmail accounts:

| Account | Credentials file | Usage |
|---|---|---|
| nityeshagarwal@gmail.com | `~/.config/gws/nityesh-personal.json` | Nityesh's personal email |
| nityesh@every.to | `~/.config/gws/nityesh-work.json` | Nityesh's work email (Every Consulting) |
| piyushagarwal5050@gmail.com | `~/.config/gws/piyush.json` | Piyush's personal email |
| luoji.claude@gmail.com | `~/.config/gws/luoji.json` | Your own email |

### CRITICAL: Every gws call MUST set `GOOGLE_WORKSPACE_CLI_CREDENTIALS_FILE`

**Never call `gws` without explicitly setting `GOOGLE_WORKSPACE_CLI_CREDENTIALS_FILE`.** Bare `gws` calls (without the env var) fall back to whichever account was last authenticated — this is unpredictable and has caused serious issues (stuck keychain prompts, wrong-inbox operations, processes piling up and overheating the machine).

Always clear the token cache **and** set the credentials file before every gws command:

```bash
# The ONLY safe pattern — use this for every gws call
rm -f ~/.config/gws/token_cache.json
GOOGLE_WORKSPACE_CLI_CREDENTIALS_FILE=~/.config/gws/<account>.json gws <command>
```

### Re-authenticating accounts

When a refresh token expires or gets revoked, re-authenticate:
1. Run `gws auth login` and complete the browser OAuth flow for the target account
2. Re-export: `gws auth export --unmasked > ~/.config/gws/<account-file>.json`

### Verifying the account

When working across multiple inboxes in a single session, verify you're on the right account before taking action:

```bash
rm -f ~/.config/gws/token_cache.json
GOOGLE_WORKSPACE_CLI_CREDENTIALS_FILE=~/.config/gws/nityesh-work.json gws gmail users getProfile --params '{"userId": "me"}'
```

### Shell aliases (for interactive use)

These are set in `~/.zshrc` but only work in interactive shells, not in Claude Code sessions. In scripts and tool calls, always use the env var + cache clear pattern above.

- `gws-personal` → nityeshagarwal@gmail.com (default)
- `gws-work` → nityesh@every.to
- `gws-piyush` → piyushagarwal5050@gmail.com
- `gws-luoji` → luoji.claude@gmail.com

## Scheduling

Use launchd (not cron) for scheduled jobs — cron can't access macOS Keychain, which Claude Code needs for authentication.

**CRITICAL — launchd uses LOCAL time, not UTC:**
- `StartCalendarInterval` `Hour` and `Minute` values are interpreted in the **machine's local timezone** (IST, UTC+5:30).
- Do NOT convert times to UTC. If you want a job to run at 8:00 AM IST, set `Hour` to `8`. Period.
- `Weekday` uses: 0 = Sunday, 1 = Monday, ..., 6 = Saturday. For Monday–Friday, use 1–5 (not 0–4).

**DO NOT "debug" or "fix" launchd plist times based on log output:**
- launchd's `log show` output can display times in misleading formats. Do NOT interpret log timestamps as evidence of a timezone offset bug in launchd — there is no such bug.
- If a job doesn't seem to fire, check: (1) is the plist loaded? (`launchctl list | grep label`), (2) is the script executable?, (3) are paths correct? Do NOT adjust Hour/Minute values to "compensate" for a perceived offset.
- **History:** A previous session misread launchd logs, concluded there was a "+12:30 offset", ran a flawed test that appeared to confirm it, and then broke all plist times. This has happened multiple times. The times in our plists are LOCAL IST — trust them.

**When scheduling Claude Code runs:**
- Always use `--dangerously-skip-permissions` — non-interactive mode can't prompt for file/tool permissions, so the job will silently fail without it.
- The Claude CLI is at `/Users/luo/.local/bin/claude` (NOT `/opt/homebrew/bin/claude`). Always use the full path in scripts.
- After loading a plist, verify the job is registered: `launchctl list | grep your-job-label`. Do NOT use `log show` timestamps to verify fire times — they can be misleading.

## Conversation history

When you need to recall past conversations, you have two sources:

**1. Claude Code session logs**
Past conversation transcripts are stored as JSONL files in `~/.claude/projects/-Users-luo/` (one file per session). To read them:
```bash
# List sessions sorted by date
ls -lt ~/.claude/projects/-Users-luo/*.jsonl

# Read a session (pipe through jq to extract human/assistant messages)
cat ~/.claude/projects/-Users-luo/<session-id>.jsonl | head -200
```
Each line is a JSON object containing the full conversation — user messages, assistant responses, and tool calls.

**2. Slack chat history**
Use the Slack SDK (see "Proactive messaging" section above) to read channel and DM history via `conversations_history`.

Between these two sources you can reconstruct the full history of what was discussed and decided.

